  [14] To find the minimum value of $X$, we need to maximize $b_1$. Since $\sum_{i=1}^n b_i = 1$, maximizing $b_1$ is equivalent to minimizing $b_i$ for $i > 1$.

By the rearrangement inequality, we can rearrange the $b_i$ such that $b_1 = 1 - \sum_{i=2}^n b_i$. To minimize $\sum_{i=2}^n b_i$, we should assign the smallest possible values to $b_i$ for $i > 1$.

Given that $n \geq 4$, we can assign $b_2 = b_3 = \cdots = b_{n-1} = 0$ and $b_n = 1 - b_1 = 1 - (1 - \sum_{i=2}^n b_i) = \sum_{i=2}^n b_i$. In this case, $X = \sum_{i=1}^n a_i b_i = a_1(1 - \sum_{i=2}^n b_i) = a_1 - a_1 \sum_{i=2}^n b_i$.

