Suppose \(f(a) = f(b) = t\) for some \(a \neq b\). Since \(f(x)\) is not injective, \(a \neq b\). Without loss of generality, assume \(a < b\). Then,

$$
f(t + y) \mid (a + f(y)) \quad \text{and} \quad f(t + y) \mid (b + f(y)).
$$

By the definition of divisibility, there exist integers \(q_1\) and \(q_2\) such that

$$
a + f(y) = q_1 f(t + y) \quad \text{and} \quad b + f(y) = q_2 f(t + y).
$$

Since \(a < b\), we have \(q_1 < q_2\). However, \(f(t + y)\) is positive for any \(y\), which implies \(q_1 < q_2 < \infty\). Thus, \(f(x)\) is bounded.

Now, we will show that the only solutions are the ones provided in the lemma.

1. If \(f(x) = x\), then \(f(x)\) is clearly injective.
2. If \(f(x)\) is not injective, then it is bounded, and since it maps \(1\) to some positive integer \(t\), it must map all positive integers to \(t\) or less. However, if \(f(x)\) maps an even number \(x > 1\) to a positive integer \(t > 1\), then \(f(x+1) > t\), which contradicts the bound on \(f(x)\). Therefore, \(f(x)\) maps even numbers to \(1\) and odd numbers to some positive integer \(t\).
3. If \(f(x)\) maps an odd number \(x > 1\) to a positive odd integer \(t > 1\), then \(f(x+1) > t\), which again contradicts the bound on \(f(x)\). Therefore, \(f(x)\) maps odd numbers to \(1\) and even numbers to \(2\).

In conclusion, the only solutions are:

1. \(f(x) = x\),
2. \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \end{cases} \) for any \(n\),
3. \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \) for any \(n\) odd.
