  [11] #### Case 1: \(f\) is bounded

Since \(f\) is bounded, let \(M\) be the maximum value of \(f\). We will show that \(f(x)=x\) or \(f(x)=1\) for all \(x\).

For \(x=1\), we have \(f(f(1)+y)\mid 1+f(y)\). Since \(f(1)=1\) and \(1+f(y)\) is always even, \(f(f(1)+y)\) must be a divisor of \(2\). The only possibility is \(f(f(1)+y)=1\), which implies \(f(x)=1\) for all \(x\).

Now, assume there exists \(k>1\) such that \(f(k)\neq 1\). Then, \(f(k)\geq 2\). Consider \(x=f(k)\). We have \(f(f(x)+y)\mid x+f(y)\), which means \(f(f(x)+y)\) must divide \(f(k)+f(y)\). Since \(f(k)\geq 2\) and \(f(y)\leq M\), \(f(f(x)+y)\leq M\). However, this contradicts the assumption that \(f(x)=f(k)\) is the maximum value of \(f\), so we must have \(f(x)=1\) for all \(x\).

#### Case 2: \(f\) is injective

In this case, we will show that \(f(x)\) must be a step function with only three possible values: \(1, 2\), or any odd number \(n\).

Let \(a\) and \(b\) be distinct positive integers such that \(f(a)=f(b)\). Then, \(f(f(a)+y)\mid a+f(y)\) and \(f(f(b)+y)\mid b+f(y)\) for any positive integer \(y\). Since \(f\) is injective, \(f(f(a)+y)\neq f(f(b)+y)\). However, both divisors must divide the same number \(a+b\), which is a contradiction. Therefore, \(f\) cannot be injective, and we have exhausted all possibilities.

In conclusion, the only functions \(f: \mathbb{Z}_+ \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}_+ \) satisfying the given condition are:
1. \(f(x) = x\),
2. \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \end{cases} \) for any \(n\),
3. \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \) for any odd \(n\).
