#### Case 2: \(f\) is not injective and bounded

Suppose \(f\) is not injective but bounded by \(M\). Since \(f\) is a function from \(\mathbb{Z}_+\) to \(\mathbb{Z}_+\), there must exist a smallest positive integer \(k\) such that \(f(k)>M\). Let \(m=\max\{f(x): x<k\}\). Then, for any \(x\in\{1,2,\dots,k-1\}\), we have \(f(x)\leq m\).

Now consider \(x=k\). Since \(f\) is not injective and \(f(k)>M\), there exists \(a<k\) such that \(f(a)=f(k)\). Without loss of generality, assume \(a<k-1\). Then,

$$
f(f(k)+y) = f(f(a)+y) \mid (k+f(y)) \quad \text{and} \quad f(f(k)+y) = f(f(k)+y) \mid (a+f(y))
$$
for any positive integer \(y\). Since \(f(k)>M\) and \(f(a)\leq m<f(k)\), we have \(f(f(k)+y)\mid (k-a)\). However, this contradicts the assumption that \(f(f(k)+y)\mid (k+f(y))\) for all \(y\).

Therefore, \(f\) must be either injective or unbounded. Since \(f\) cannot be unbounded (as it maps \(\mathbb{Z}_+\) to \(\mathbb{Z}_+\)), we conclude that \(f(x)=x\) is the only solution when \(f\) is injective, and \(f\) cannot be bounded.

#### Case 3: \(f\) is not injective and unbounded

Let \(f(1)=n\). Since \(f\) is not injective, there exists \(a>1\) such that \(f(a)=n\). Define \(g(x)=f(x)-n\). Then, \(g(1)=0\) and \(g(a)=0\), which implies \(g\) is not injective. Moreover, \(g\) is unbounded since \(f\) is unbounded.

By Case 2, \(g\) must be of the form \(g(x)=x\) for \(x\leq a\), and \(g(x)=1\) for \(x>a\). Therefore, \(f(x)=n+g(x)=n+x\) for \(x\leq a\), and \(f(x)=n+1\) for \(x>a\).

Now, let \(a=2^k\) for some \(k\in\mathbb{Z}_+\). We claim that \(f\) is of the form:

$$
f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases}
$$
for any odd \(n\).

To prove this, note that \(f(a)=n\) and \(f(a+1)=n+1\). Since \(a\) is a power of 2, \(a+1\) is odd. Now, let \(x=a+2^i\) for some \(i\in\mathbb{Z}_+\). If \(i=0\), then \(x=a+1\), and \(f(x)=n+1\) as claimed. If \(i>0\), then \(x\) is even, and \(f(x)=f(a+2^i)=f(a+2^{i-1}+2)=f(a+2^{i-1})+1\). Since \(a+2^{i-1}\) is odd, \(f(a+2^{i-1})=n+1\) by the induction hypothesis. Thus, \(f(x)=n+2=2\) for even \(x\).

Since \(f\) is not injective, there exists \(b>a\) such that \(f(b)=n\). Let \(b=a+2^j\) for some \(j>k\). Then, \(f(b)=f(a+2^j)=f(a+2^{j-1}+2^{k+1})=f(a+2^{k+1})+2^{j-k-1}\). Since \(a+2^{k+1}\) is odd, \(f(a+2^{k+1})=n+1\) by the induction hypothesis. Thus, \(f(b)=n+2^{j-k-1}\), which is not equal to \(n\) for \(j>k\). This contradicts the assumption that \(f(b)=n\). Therefore, our initial assumption that \(f\) is not injective and unbounded leads to a contradiction, and the only solutions are:

1. \(f(x) = x\),
2. \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \end{cases}\) for any \(n\),
3. \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases}\) for any odd \(n\).
  [13] **Claim.** \(f(x) = x\) is the only possible solution when \(f\) is injective.

Proof:
Since \(f\) is injective, \(f(1)\) is unique. Suppose \(f(1) > 1\). Then, by the lemma, \(f(1 + y) = f(f(1) + y) \mid (1 + f(y))\). Since \(f(1) > 1\), there exists a \(y\) such that \(1 + f(y) > f(1)\), which contradicts the injectivity of \(f\). Therefore, \(f(1) = 1\).

Now, for \(x > 1\), we have \(f(x) = f(1 + (x - 1)) \mid 1 + (f(x - 1) - 1) = f(x - 1)\). Since \(f\) is injective, \(f(x) = x\) for all \(x > 1\). Hence, \(f(x) = x\) is the only possible solution when \(f\) is injective.

#### Case 2: \(f\) is bounded
We will show that \(f(x) = 1\) for all \(x\) in this case.

Proof:
Since \(f\) is bounded, let \(M = \max\{f(x) : x \in \mathbb{Z}_+\}\). We will prove by contradiction that \(f(x) = 1\) for all \(x\).

Suppose \(f(x_0) > 1\) for some \(x_0\). Then, \(f(x_0 + y) = f(f(x_0) + y) \mid (x_0 + f(y))\). Since \(f(x_0) > 1\), there exists a \(y\) such that \(f(y) < M - f(x_0)\). Then, \(f(x_0 + y) \leq M - f(x_0) < M\), which contradicts the definition of \(M\) as the maximum value of \(f\). Therefore, \(f(x) = 1\) for all \(x\).

#### Case 3: \(f\) is neither injective nor bounded
We will show that \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \) for any odd \(n\) is the only possible solution when \(f\) is neither injective nor bounded.

Proof:
Suppose \(f(x_0) \neq 1\) for some \(x_0 > 1\). Since \(f\) is not bounded, there exists a sequence \((x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}\) such that \(x_n > x_{n+1}\) and \(f(x_n) \to \infty\) as \(n \to \infty\).

Let \(x_0 = 2k + 1\) for some \(k \in \mathbb{N}\). Since \(f(x_0) \neq 1\), there exists a \(y_0 \in \mathbb{N}\) such that \(f(y_0) > x_0\). Now, consider the sequence \((y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}\) defined by \(y_n = f(y_{n-1}) - y_{n-1}\). Since \(f(y_{n-1}) > x_0 > y_{n-1}\), we have \(y_n > 0\) for all \(n\). Furthermore, since \(f\) is not injective, there exists a \(y_m \neq y_l\) for some \(m, l \in \mathbb{N}\). Let \(n = \max\{m,l\}\). Then, \(y_n = f(y_{n-1}) - y_{n-1} = f(y_{n-2}) - f(y_{n-3}) + y_{n-3} - y_{n-2} = \dots = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} (f(y_{i-1}) - f(y_i)) + y_0\).

Since \(f(y_i) \to \infty\) as \(i \to \infty\), there exists an \(N \in \mathbb{N}\) such that \(f(y_i) > x_i\) for all \(i > N\). Therefore, \(\sum_{i=1}^{N} (f(y_{i-1}) - f(y_i)) < \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} (x_i - y_i) = x_0\). Since \(y_N > 0\), we have \(y_N < x_0\). However, this contradicts the definition of \(y_N\) since \(y_N = f(y_{N-1}) > x_0 > y_N\). Therefore, \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \) for any odd \(n\) is the only possible solution when \(f\) is neither injective nor bounded.

In conclusion, the only solutions are:
1. \(f(x) = x\),
2. \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \end{cases} \) for any \(n\),
3. \(f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \) for any odd \(n\).
