Suppose, for the sake of contradiction, that there exists $1 \leq k \leq M$ such that $f(k) > k$. Since $f$ is bounded by $M$, we have $f(k) \leq M$. Now, consider the sequence $k, f(k), f(f(k)), \dots$. By the definition of $f$, each term in this sequence is a divisor of the next one. Moreover, since $f(k) > k$, the sequence strictly increases. However, this sequence cannot have more than $M$ distinct terms, since $M$ is the maximum value of $f$. This contradicts the fact that the sequence strictly increases.

Therefore, $f(k) = k$ for all $1 \leq k \leq M$. Now, we can use strong induction to show that $f(x) = x$ for all $x \geq 1$. The base case $x = 1$ is already proven. Suppose $f(x) = x$ for all $x = 1, \dots, n$. Plugging in $(x, y) = (n + 1, 1)$ in the original equation, we get:

$$
f(f(n + 1) + 1) \mid (n + 1 + f(1)) = f(f(n + 1) + 1) \mid (n + 2).
$$

If $f(f(n + 1) + 1) > n + 2$, then the sequence $n + 1, f(n + 1), f(f(n + 1)), \dots$ would have more than $M$ distinct terms, which contradicts the fact that $f(n + 1) \leq M$. Therefore, $f(f(n + 1) + 1) \leq n + 2$. Since $f(n + 1) = n + 1$ by the induction hypothesis, we have $f(f(n + 1) + 1) \leq n + 2 \leq f(n + 2)$. However, since $f$ is injective, this implies $f(n + 2) = f(f(n + 1) + 1) = n + 2$. Thus, $f(n + 2) = n + 2$ for all $n \geq 1$, completing the induction.

In summary, the only solutions to the problem are $f(x) = x$ and $f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \end{cases}$ for any $n$.
